---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../components/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import CaseStudyHero from '../../components/CaseStudyHero.astro';
import CaseStudySection from '../../components/case-study/CaseStudySection.astro';
import CaseStudySubsection from '../../components/case-study/CaseStudySubsection.astro';

const sections = [
    { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
    { id: 'problem', label: 'Problem' },
    { id: 'constraints', label: 'Constraints' },
    { id: 'exploration', label: 'Exploration' },
    { id: 'decision', label: 'Decision' },
    { id: 'solution', label: 'Solution' },
    { id: 'outcome', label: 'Outcome' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout title="Lingotune — Julia Heo" sections={sections}>
	<CaseStudyHero
		logo="/images/lingotune/logo.png"
		logoAlt="Lingotune"
		headline="Making Lingotune's song library feel discoverable"
		role="Product Design Intern"
		timeframe="May – August 2025"
		tags={['Product Design', 'Mobile', 'EdTech']}
	/>

	<CaseStudySection id="overview" title="Overview">
	<h2 class="section-tagline">Lingotune had a library of songs. Users thought it only had one.</h2>
		<p>
    <a href="https://www.lingotune.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-link">Lingotune</a> helps people learn languages through music, pairing songs with short lessons that make vocabulary and grammar easier to remember.
		</p>
		<p>
			However, users arriving on the song selection screen often saw the first card and stopped there. The rest of the library technically existed, it just wasn't visible and intuitive.
		</p>
		<p>
			In under a week, I redesigned the browsing experience, from a single static card to a browsable song library. The new interaction shipped in Lingotune's next release.
		</p>

		<div class="before-after">
			<div class="before-after__item">
				<img src="/images/lingotune/oldold.png" alt="Original song selection screen showing a single song card with small arrows" class="case-study-image case-study-image--phone" />
				<p class="before-after__label">Before</p>
			</div>
			<div class="before-after__item">
				<img src="/images/lingotune/preview.png" alt="Shipped redesign: horizontal carousel with a peeking next card and pagination dots" class="case-study-image case-study-image--phone" />
				<p class="before-after__label before-after__label--after">After</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="problem" title="Problem">
		<h2 class="section-tagline">The interface showed a song, not a collection.</h2>
		<p>The original experience displayed one song card at a time, with small arrows for moving between options. Functionally, users could browse. Visually, almost nothing suggested that they should.</p>
		<p>Reviewing and analyzing over 20 UXCam test sessions revealed two repeated behaviors:</p>
		<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">01</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Users tried to swipe</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Before looking for another control, users tried to swipe the card horizontally, a gesture they already associated with browsing a collection on mobile.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">02</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Users stopped at the first song</p>
        <p class="observation__text">When swiping did nothing and the arrows went unnoticed, many users didn't continue exploring. The screen looked complete, so the first card felt like the only available option.</p>
    </div>

</div>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/user-test-review.png" alt="UXCam session footage showing a user swiping the song card with no response from the interface" class="case-study-image" />
		<div class="reframe">
			<p class="reframe__final"> 🤔 How might I make the library itself feel browsable?</p>
		</div>

	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="constraints" title="Constraints">
    <h2 class="section-tagline">Improve discoverability without rebuilding the entire screen.</h2>

    <div class="constraint-grid">
        <div class="constraint-card">
            <p class="constraint-card__label">⏳ Under one week</p>
            <p class="constraint-card__text">Users were already running into the issue, so we needed a direction that could be designed, approved, and prepared for development quickly.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="constraint-card">
            <p class="constraint-card__label"> 🎶 Keep the existing song cards</p>
            <p class="constraint-card__text">The cards and their content structure were already established. I needed to improve the way users moved between them without redesigning each card.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="constraint-card">
            <p class="constraint-card__label"> 🗒️ Use the existing design system</p>
            <p class="constraint-card__text">The new interaction still had to feel native to Lingotune and remain realistic for the team to implement.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="constraint-card">
            <p class="constraint-card__label"> 👤👤👤 Small team, quick decisions</p>
            <p class="constraint-card__text">I worked alongside another designer and the founder, which meant sharing early explorations, narrowing the direction quickly, and keeping the solution focused.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="exploration" title="Exploration">
		<h2 class="section-tagline">I explored how much of the experience actually needed to change.</h2>
		<p>I quickly sketched three directions, from a small interface adjustment to a larger change in how songs were presented.</p>
		<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">01</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Make the arrows more prominent</p>
        <p class="observation__text">This was the fastest change, but it still asked users to notice and learn a control they were already overlooking. It treated the symptom without fixing the screen's lack of discoverability.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">02</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Turn the library into a vertical feed</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Showing several songs at once would make the library more visible, but it introduced another vertical browsing pattern into an already vertically scrolling page. Songs and page content would compete for the same gesture.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">03</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Use a horizontal carousel</p>
        <p class="observation__text">A carousel preserved the existing cards, kept song browsing separate from page scrolling, and supported the swipe gesture users were already attempting.</p>
    </div>
</div>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/brainstorming.png" alt="Sketches comparing three exploration directions: larger arrows, vertical feed, and horizontal carousel" class="case-study-image" />

	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="decision" title="Decision">
		<h2 class="section-tagline">Instead of teaching users a new behavior, I designed around the one they already had.</h2>
		<p>I moved forward with the horizontal carousel because it addressed both sides of the problem: it made browsing feel familiar, and it made the existence of additional songs visible before users interacted.</p>
		<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">01</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">How do users browse?</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Swipe between songs.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">02</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">How do they know there's more?</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Show part of the next card.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">03</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">How do they know where they are?</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Provide lightweight pagination.</p>
    </div>
</div>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/preview.png" alt="Shipped carousel design showing the centered song card, a peeking next card, and pagination dots" class="case-study-image case-study-image--phone" />
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="solution" title="Solution">
		<h3 class="solution-block-heading">01 Make browsing feel immediate</h3>
		<p>Users could now swipe horizontally between songs, the same gesture I repeatedly saw them attempt in test sessions. This removed the need to find a separate control before exploring because the interaction matched what users already expected the card to do.</p>
		<video src="/images/lingotune/demo.mp4" autoplay loop muted playsinline class="mockup-video"></video>
		<h3 class="solution-block-heading">02 Let the next song enter the frame</h3>
		<p>I exposed part of the neighboring card so the interface no longer appeared to end after the first song. Rather than relying on instructions or a more prominent arrow, the layout itself communicated that the library continued.</p>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/1designdecision.png" alt="Close-up comparing a fully centered card with no peeking edge to the redesigned layout showing part of the next card" class="case-study-image case-study-image--wide" />
		<h3 class="solution-block-heading">03 Give users a sense of progress</h3>
		<p>Pagination dots provided lightweight feedback as users moved through the library.</p>
		<p>The side peeking cards answered "is there more?" The pagination dots answered "where am I?"</p>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/2designdecision.png" alt="Three-song sequence showing pagination dots updating as the user swipes between songs" class="case-study-image case-study-image--wide" />
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="outcome" title="Outcome">
		<h2 class="section-tagline">From observed behavior to a shipped redesign, in under a week.</h2>
		<p>
			The team approved the direction, and the redesigned song browsing experience shipped in Lingotune's next release.
		</p>
		<p> I didn't have access to post-launch analytics, so I can't claim a measured change in engagement. If I were evaluating the release, I would focus on three signals: </p>
		<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">01</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Browse depth</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Are more users moving beyond the first song?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">02</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Lesson starts</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Does exploring more of the library lead to more lesson selections?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">03</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Screen exit rate</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Are fewer users leaving before choosing a song?</p>
    </div>
</div>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/nishoutcome.png" alt="Final shipped song browsing experience with multiple song cards and a swipe indicator" class="case-study-image case-study-image--wide" />
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="outcome" title="Reflection">
		<h2 class="section-tagline">Sometimes, the clearest design direction is already hiding in user behavior.</h2>
		<p>I entered the project thinking the navigation control needed to be fixed. Watching real sessions changed how I framed the problem: users didn't just need a better way to move forward — they needed to understand that there was somewhere to go.</p>
		<p>This project reminded me that familiar interaction patterns are useful for more than usability. When used intentionally, they can also communicate what a product contains and what users can do next.</p>
		<p class="reflection-closing">The behavior didn't need to be invented. It needed to be supported.</p>
	</CaseStudySection>


</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
	.before-after {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: center;
		align-items: flex-end;
		gap: 32px;
		margin-top: 24px;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
	}

	.before-after__item {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
	}

	.before-after__item .case-study-image--phone {
		margin: 0;
	}

	.before-after__item:first-child .case-study-image--phone {
		opacity: 0.55;
		transform: scale(0.92);
	}

	.before-after__label {
		margin: 10px 0 0;
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-size: 13px;
		font-weight: 500;
		color: #868686;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.before-after__label--after {
		color: #1a1a1a;
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.before-after__caption {
		margin-top: 12px;
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-size: 13px;
		color: #868686;
		text-align: center;
		font-style: italic;
	}

	.problem-annotation {
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-style: italic;
		color: #4a4a4a;
	}

	.reframe {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 10px;
		margin: 24px auto 8px;
		padding: 24px;
		max-width: 480px;
		border-radius: 12px;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.reframe__initial {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-size: 15px;
		color: #868686;
	}

	.reframe__arrow {
		font-size: 18px;
		color: #c4c4c4;
	}

	.reframe__final {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-size: 17px;
		font-weight: 600;
		color: #4483F0;
	}

	.constraints-note {
		margin-top: 16px;
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-size: 14px;
		font-style: italic;
		color: #868686;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.flow-line {
		margin: 20px 0 0;
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-size: 15px;
		font-weight: 600;
		color: #4483F0;
		text-align: center;
		letter-spacing: 0.01em;
	}

	.flow-line span {
		color: #c4c4c4;
		font-weight: 400;
		margin: 0 4px;
	}

	.reflection-closing {
		font-family: 'Gabarito', sans-serif;
		font-weight: 600;
		font-size: 17px;
		color: #1a1a1a;
	}

	.mockup-video {
		display: block;
		max-height: 500px;
		width: auto;
		margin: 24px auto;
		border-radius: 12px;
	}

	@media (max-width: 768px) {
		.mockup-video {
			max-height: 280px;
			width: 100%;
			max-width: 100%;
		}

		.before-after {
			gap: 16px;
		}
	}
</style>
